  <section class="case-heading wrap"><a class="back" href="/projects/">All projects</a><p class="eyebrow">Vue, FastAPI, SQLite and workflow rules</p><h1>Field Service<br>Workflow</h1><p class="hero-description">A fictional workshop application for turning a device fault into a traceable work order, from assignment through resolution.</p><div class="case-facts"><div><span>Project</span><strong>Personal project · October 2026</strong></div><div><span>Data</span><strong>Synthetic assets and work orders</strong></div><div><span>Verification</span><strong>37 API tests · 27 demo tests</strong></div></div><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/field-service-workflow/">Open interactive demo</a><a class="text-link" href="/downloads/field-service-workflow.zip" download>Download source</a></div></section>
  <section class="wrap section standalone-case"><h2>Keep the record and its history together.</h2><p>A status field alone cannot explain who changed a work order or why. This project applies explicit status rules and writes each accepted change with an event in the same SQLite transaction.</p><figure><img src="/media/workflow-desktop.png" alt="Synthetic work order in Closed state with its five-step activity history" loading="lazy" width="1440" height="1705"><figcaption>Browser acceptance run of the static demo, using synthetic records and technician labels.</figcaption></figure>
  <h2>Two implementations, one set of rules.</h2><p>The local application uses a real FastAPI service and SQLite database. The public demo runs entirely in the current browser tab and resets on refresh. Both read the same status transition contract; the public version does not claim database persistence.</p><div class="architecture"><span>Open → Assigned → In Progress</span><b>↓</b><span>Resolved → Closed</span><span>Resolved may return to Open</span></div><p>Assignment requires a technician label. Resolution requires a treatment note. A closed work order cannot be reopened. Reopening a resolved order clears its current assignment and resolution while retaining the earlier history.</p>
  <h2>Make conflicting and failed writes visible.</h2><p>The API requires the version the user last read. If another change has already advanced that version, it rejects the stale update. The interface keeps input when a request fails and offers a record reload.</p><p>A database test deliberately blocks an event insert. It verifies that the work order change also rolls back, preventing a new status without its history. Separate tests exercise all 25 possible origin and target status pairs.</p><div class="stats"><div><strong>37</strong><span>API tests passed</span></div><div><strong>27</strong><span>browser-service unit tests passed</span></div><div><strong>2</strong><span>browser modes accepted</span></div></div><p>Browser checks completed the create–assign–work–resolve–close sequence in both modes. They also verified four viewport widths, required notes, history, filtering, reset, database persistence and network failure handling.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/workflow-browser-results.json" download>Download browser check results</a>
  <h2>Scope and contribution.</h2><p>The implementation includes a shared contract, API, transaction handling, front-end service separation, interface and tests. It uses no employer code or old project implementation.</p><p>This is a learning application for a synthetic business scenario. Actor labels are not authenticated identities. Production authorisation, tamper-resistant history, attachments, pagination and multi-user browser persistence have not been implemented.</p><h2>Run the complete application.</h2><pre>python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
python -m uvicorn backend.app:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8001</pre><p>The source README explains the Vue API mode, independent database paths and reproduction commands. Times are stored in UTC and displayed explicitly in Perth time.</p>
